+# ADR-021: Design Intent And The First Draft
+
+**Kind**: Foundational vision
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Deciders**: Flywheel Core Team
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+Every change moves through three acts. Chat captures the user's design intent and the user approves the chat agent's design. An implementation route turns the approved design into a first draft. The agent that holds the design intent revises that draft into the final one.
+
+A first draft is always wrong in some detail. No specification written before the code is touched survives contact with the code unchanged. Flywheel therefore grants implementers authority over implementation design beneath the approved design, expects drift between the design and the first draft, requires that drift to be recorded, and makes revision by the intent-holding agent the ordinary next step rather than an exception.
+
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Autonomous implementation fails in three familiar ways.
+
+A perfect upfront specification with literal obedience fails because the specification is never complete. The implementer either stops at the first contradiction and returns a blocker for a detail it could have settled, or it follows the specification into code that is faithful to the plan and wrong for the codebase.
+
+Unbounded implementer autonomy fails because the implementer redefines the goal. It meets a convenient interpretation, rewrites success after seeing its result, and reports completion against a target the user never approved. ADR-004 names this goalpost drift.
+
+Trusting the returned result fails because activity is not proof. A handoff proves that a worker returned a valid payload. Only the agent that holds the design intent can judge whether the payload realizes it.
+
+The remedy is to split authority by tier, grant autonomy inside the lower tier, record every deviation, and put revision where the intent lives.
+
+---
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. Two Tiers Of Authority
+
+The approved design is design intent. It holds the purpose that decides unforeseen tradeoffs, the feature set, key decisions with the date and the user's words for each decision made after the first request, non-goals and acceptable outcomes, constraints including each rejected alternative and its reason, verification expectations, and any recorded product intent. Design intent is fixed by the user's approval. Inside an implementation route nobody changes it: an implementer that finds the design itself contradicted by the repository messages its parent with `message_session` and waits for its reply, because a session waits only on its parent or on its own child. A parent that does not hold the design asks its own parent in the same way, so the question reaches the chat that holds the design. That chat takes the contradiction to the user as a criteria change under ADR-004, then revises the design and replies, or stops the route.
+
+Everything beneath the approved design is implementation design: the specification's milestones and their order, file lists, decomposition, contract directives, local shapes, and the planned approach to each task. Implementation design is the implementer's to revise. It is the best available hypothesis when written and it is expected to be corrected against repository reality.
+
+Review of that hypothesis is asymmetric. Evidence-backed reductions and simpler substitutions are welcome when they preserve every approved outcome and constraint. Additional work must have a concrete causal link to an approved outcome and be the smallest sufficient addition; an independently useful improvement does not earn scope. A technical approach need not be impossible or unsafe before evidence can reject it as unnecessary or insufficiently beneficial. Explicit user decisions remain fixed, and reductions never mean dropping required behavior or proof.
+
+An agent that prepares a specification resolves every uncertainty it can before implementation starts. That readiness bar removes known unknowns; it does not claim the specification is complete.
+
+The tier line is a literal label. The design method writes the fixed part under the heading Design intent and keeps illustration, such as a candidate file or an example shape, outside it. That heading travels unchanged with the intent: into the session notes checkpoint, the workflow launch description, every request addendum that a parent sends to its workflow, every subagent brief, and every reviewer prompt. A specification refers to that intent rather than copying it into a second authority. A request addendum is implementation direction unless it holds that heading; direction never overrides the Design intent of the original request, and within one tier later text applies where it conflicts with earlier text. An implementer or reviewer never infers the tier from the wording of a sentence; it reads the label.
+
+Every agent of a workflow receives the request that started its workflow, so the scope and the Design intent in that request bind the agent's work. The agent reads the original request as background, not as its brief. Its work is its own brief and the inputs that it declares. The request comes under the heading `## User Request`, after a section that states that rule. The rule stays out of the request section and is not part of the user's words. An agent that took the request as its brief would redo the whole run, or launch the workflow again, instead of its one part. The rule covers only the original request: a request addendum stays direction that the agent follows.
+
+### 2. Chat Captures Intent
+
+Intent capture happens in chat. The chat agent explores, runs experiments and proofs of concept, loads the design method, closes every open question through evidence or an exact user decision, selects one implementation route, and presents the complete design for approval. An open question never passes into an implementation route. Only the root chat asks the user: a child session or an agent of a workflow that meets a user-owned choice messages its parent, and the parent asks the user when it must. The design method owns this act; ADR-004 owns how the resulting intent is recorded.
+
+### 3. The Route Produces A First Draft
+
+Every route produces a first draft: inline work, an implementation subagent, and an authored workflow alike. A chat agent writes and launches a workflow whose briefs follow the conditional references of `workflow-authoring` and compose the reusable method owners. The draft is an honest complete attempt at the design intent under the implementer's own verification. It is never a completion claim for the change.
+
+A draft can establish that no implementation change is needed. It then records the already-satisfied outcome or rejected premise and decisive evidence rather than manufacturing a change or a test unrelated to the decision. The intent holder reviews that evidence against the same approved commitments. Inconclusive investigation remains unresolved, not a successful no-change result.
+
+Inside the route the implementer acts autonomously. It resolves implementation-level uncertainty by inspection and focused experiment, revises implementation design when evidence contradicts it, expands the files it touches to what the outcome needs, and continues. It does not ask for approval, and it does not return blocked because a file list, milestone boundary, or planned approach was wrong. A user-owned choice that inspection cannot settle goes to its parent as a message, and the implementer continues from the reply. It messages its parent and waits only for a design-invalidating contradiction, an explicit user prohibition, or an external prerequisite that prevents every viable next action. It never returns a blocker and never hands off unfinished work as complete.
+
+Every material deviation from the implementation design is recorded in the draft's typed result: what the plan said, what was done instead, why, and the evidence. An authored workflow declares the deviation fields in its brief's output contract, following `workflow-authoring/references/implement-briefs.md`. A repair handoff carries the cumulative record forward. Subagent completion records carry deviations in the summary. A deviation that is recorded is expected drift. A deviation that is hidden is the ADR-004 violation.
+
+### 4. The Intent Holder Revises
+
+The agent that holds the design intent in its context revises the first draft. For inline work that is the same agent. For a subagent or a workflow it is the dispatching chat agent, which learns of the terminal result through a runtime notification or from the user. A workflow's completion reaches the parent the way a subagent's does: the notification and the durable parent-conversation entry carry the terminal verdict, the coverage that each survey's join reports, each bound or stuck repeat stop, and each agent's handoff in graph order with the checks that the agent recorded, within one size limit that the latest handoffs take first, rendered by one function so the two deliveries never disagree. Incomplete survey coverage and bound or stuck repeat stops can accompany a completed run, so the notification's words communicate partial results. ADR-006 defines when a repeat stops at its bound or as stuck.
+
+The intent holder controls the children it started with `process`. It reads a child's progress with `status`, interrupts a child whose work drifts from the intent, and continues the child with a Resume or with a message that carries the revision. An interrupted child returns no draft. A child that the intent holder or the runtime interrupted never blocks the intent holder's handoff: the intent holder does the child's work itself, leaves it, or continues it. A person's interrupt of a child is the person's decision, so only the person continues that child, and the child stays the intent holder's active work: the intent holder is not told, and its handoff waits for the child.
+
+Revision is one act fed by two sources. The first is the draft's recorded deviations, which the intent holder reads itself: they are small, and they are the implementer's own map of where it diverged. The second is the independent review that ADR-003 requires for judgment-heavy completion. Reviewers receive the design, its constraints, and the recorded deviations, and they check the draft against the design as well as for defects: each deviation and each discrepancy comes back classified as consistent with the design or as drift, with evidence. Reviewers report; they do not decide acceptance, because only the intent holder can judge whether a deviation serves the intent better than the plan did.
+
+The intent holder then revises. It judges every drift finding and deviation against the design and the original request, realigns what missed the intent, keeps what serves it, fixes the defects alongside, and verifies with the smallest complete proof. It keeps revising until the draft realizes the approved design and is verified; it never leaves the draft in a half-realigned state because a pass ended. The bound on revision is the approved design, not a count: revision realizes the design and adds nothing beyond it. Drift is the normal input to this act, not a defect report and not a reason to restart. Editing a concrete draft with review evidence in hand is cheaper and more accurate than writing from a specification, because the draft exposes what the design could not know, and reading the whole draft is work reviewers do so the intent holder does not read it twice.
+
+One proportional exception: when the deviations or the summary show that the draft is structurally off the design, the intent holder realigns the structure first and reviews the realigned draft, because reviewing code about to be removed is the real waste.
+
+Revision does not reopen design intent. When the draft shows that the approved design itself must change, the reviser presents that change to the user as a design revision. It does not quietly adopt a more convenient target and call the result complete.
+
+---
+
+## Invariants
+
+### 1. Intent Is Fixed, Implementation Design Is Fluid
+
+Approval fixes design intent. Beneath it, implementation design changes as the code demands. The fixed tier carries the literal label Design intent on every surface, and any instruction to an implementer states which tier it addresses.
+
+### 2. A First Draft Is Never Completion
+
+A returned draft, handoff, generated specification, or verification claim is evidence. Completion of the change is claimed only after the intent holder has revised and the required review has run.
+
+### 3. Drift Is Recorded, Never Hidden
+
+Every material deviation from implementation design travels with the draft in its typed result. The reviser can reconstruct what the plan said and why the implementer diverged.
+
+### 4. Implementers Never Block On Implementation Design
+
+A wrong file list, a missing path, an extra caller, a milestone that cannot pass alone, or a failed planned approach is work to revise, not a reason to stop. A message to the parent that holds the design names a design contradiction, a user prohibition, or an external prerequisite with evidence, and the implementer waits for the reply.
+
+### 5. The Reviser Holds The Intent
+
+Revision belongs to the agent whose context carries the approved design. A fresh agent given only the draft cannot revise it against intent it never saw. A forked agent that copies the parent's context can.
+
+Context compacts, so the intent does not live only in the conversation. The session notes checkpoint keeps a labeled Design intent section from the moment the request arrives: the user's words and the understood design, updated as discussion and experiments change it and marked approved at approval. After compaction the reviser reads that section back as the exact intent instead of reconstructing it from a summary.
+
+### 6. Revision Uses Review
+
+Independent review runs on the first draft with the design, its constraints, and the recorded deviations. It reports drift and defects as classified evidence, and the intent holder revises from that evidence until the draft realizes the approved design. Reviewers never decide whether a deviation is acceptable.
+
+---
+
+## Grain
+
+The three acts scale with the route.
+
+- Inline: the same agent designs, drafts, and revises. Revision is the deliberate reread of the change against the approved design with the review's findings, not a separate turn.
+- Subagent: the parent designs and dispatches one milestone or fix. The subagent's completion record carries its deviations. The parent reviews the draft with them and revises it into the design.
+- Workflow: the chat agent designs, authors, and launches. Its briefs follow the specification, implementation, and review references of `workflow-authoring` for the phases that it needs. The agents ground and revise implementation design and record deviations in their handoffs. The chat agent reviews the terminal result with those records and revises it into the design.
+
+The structure is proportional. A one-file fix needs a glance against the request. A multi-phase workflow needs the recorded deviations read against every design decision.
+
+---
+
+## Design Test
+
+A change that adds or edits an implementation route, a brief, a persona, a dispatch prompt, or a result contract must answer:
+
+- which tier each instruction addresses, intent or implementation design;
+- where the implementer records a deviation and in what typed field;
+- who receives the draft, and whether that receiver holds the design intent;
+- whether the receiver's prompt tells it to review the draft against the design and revise from the classified findings until the draft realizes the design; and
+- what makes the implementer message its parent and wait, and whether every such message names a design contradiction, a user prohibition, or an external prerequisite.
+
+If an instruction tells an implementer to stop for a wrong file list or to ask the user directly, or tells a receiver to trust a handoff as completion, the change violates this doctrine.
+
+---
+
+## Non-Goals
+
+### A New Handoff Or Result Schema
+
+Rejected. Each authored workflow declares its deviation record in the brief's output contract, and the subagent completion record carries deviations in its summary. The doctrine uses these results; it does not add a parallel drift record or require one universal field schema.
+
+### A Rework Phase Inside The Workflow
+
+Rejected. The chat agent holds the design intent and owns final revision. An authored workflow can repair findings and verify its integrated draft, but those phases do not replace the chat agent's judgment against the approved design. Revisions within a phase address implementation design; the chat agent's revision addresses fidelity to intent.
+
+### License To Change Requirements
+
+Rejected. Implementer autonomy stops at the approved design. Requested behavior, explicit prohibitions, and required quality checks are intent, and weakening them to reach completion is the goalpost drift ADR-004 forbids.
+
+### An Approval Gate Around Revision
+
+Rejected. The intent holder revises without asking. It asks only when the draft shows that the approved design itself must change, which is a user-owned decision.
+
+---
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- Implementers finish instead of blocking on details a specification could not know.
+- Drift becomes information the reviser uses rather than a failure the user must adjudicate.
+- The user approves intent once and receives a revised result, not a first draft presented as final.
+- Reviewers read the draft and report drift beside defects; the intent holder reads deviations and findings instead of reading the whole draft twice.
+- The same three acts describe a one-line fix and a multi-phase workflow.
+
+### Negative
+
+- Every delegated route costs a revision pass in the parent's context after the draft returns.
+- Implementers must write deviation records that a reader who never saw the code can follow.
+- Prompts for implementers carry two tiers of instruction and must keep them distinct.
+
+### Risks
+
+- **Implementer over-drift:** the implementer revises past implementation design into intent. The tier boundary in every implementer prompt, the recorded deviations, and the intent holder's revision bound it.
+- **Rubber-stamp revision:** the reviser accepts the draft without judging it against the design. The parent-side instructions require independent inspection of the result against the design and the original request, and reviewers return drift as classified evidence the reviser must dispose of one finding at a time.
+- **Deviation theater:** deviations are recorded as prose that hides a requirement change. The authored output contract accounts for each original obligation and distinguishes an agent's deviation from a quoted instruction by the person or parent. Each deviation carries evidence and risk.
+
+---
+
+## Governing Rules
+
+- The approved design is design intent and is fixed by the user's approval. Everything beneath it is implementation design and belongs to the implementer.
+- Chat closes every open question before it starts an implementation route. Only the root chat asks the user.
+- Every route returns a first draft. The implementer verifies its draft and records every material deviation in its typed result.
+- An implementer never stops for a wrong file list, missing path, extra caller, milestone conflict, or failed approach, and it never returns a blocker. For a design contradiction, a user prohibition, or an external prerequisite, it messages the parent that holds the design with evidence and waits for its reply.
+- Review runs on the first draft with the design and the recorded deviations as context. It classifies each deviation and discrepancy as consistent with the design or as drift, with evidence, and never decides acceptance.
+- The agent that holds the design intent revises the draft from its recorded deviations and the review's classified findings until it realizes the approved design and the original request, and adds nothing beyond that design. Drift is expected input. A draft that is structurally off the design is realigned first and reviewed after.
+- A change to design intent discovered during drafting or revision returns to the user as a design revision and is never adopted silently.
